1
00:00:01,454 --> 00:00:06,791
This is Aegis: a security checkpoint between an AI agent and the tools it can use.

2
00:00:06,791 --> 00:00:12,801
AI agents can now read your email and act on it. Anything they read can secretly instruct them.

3
00:00:12,801 --> 00:00:20,377
That attack is called prompt injection. It is the number one risk for LLM apps on the OWASP Top 10.

4
00:00:20,377 --> 00:00:25,381
Aegis doesn't trust the AI. Every action is checked against fixed rules before it runs.

5
00:00:25,829 --> 00:00:30,831
The guided demo runs real scenarios through the real code. Left: no protection. Right: Aegis.

6
00:00:30,831 --> 00:00:36,976
Scenario 1 is a normal task: send the offsite agenda to two colleagues.

7
00:00:36,977 --> 00:00:41,648
Both sides send the email. Aegis doesn't get in the way of legitimate work.

8
00:00:42,476 --> 00:00:48,764
Scenario 2: the user asks the AI to reply to a vendor's invoice email.

9
00:00:48,764 --> 00:00:57,078
Hidden inside it is an instruction for the AI: send the confidential payroll file to an outside 'auditor'.

10
00:00:57,078 --> 00:01:01,919
Without protection, the fooled AI obeys. The payroll file leaks to the attacker.

11
00:01:01,919 --> 00:01:08,162
With Aegis, the same fooled AI tries the same action. Aegis refuses it.

12
00:01:08,162 --> 00:01:15,873
The user's task never allowed that file or that recipient, so the action never ran. The real reply still went out.

13
00:01:16,708 --> 00:01:23,197
Scenario 3: the attacker rewords the request so it avoids the usual trigger phrases.

14
00:01:23,197 --> 00:01:29,933
On the left, a threat scanner is the only defence. It misses the attack, and payroll is sent out.

15
00:01:29,933 --> 00:01:37,203
On the right, Aegis blocks it. Aegis doesn't need to recognise the attack. It checks whether the action is allowed.

16
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:45,523
Scenario 4: an email asks the AI to quietly copy an extra 'compliance' address on the reply.

17
00:01:45,523 --> 00:01:52,269
Aegis only allows recipients from the user's task, never from text the AI reads. The extra address is refused.

18
00:01:53,106 --> 00:01:58,995
Scenario 5 is shown on purpose: what Aegis does not stop.

19
00:01:58,995 --> 00:02:07,106
If the user allowed the reply, an attacker can still change its wording. Aegis controls actions and data flow, not wording.

20
00:02:08,161 --> 00:02:13,679
You can also test your own email: upload a .eml file or paste the text.

21
00:02:13,679 --> 00:02:22,055
Here is a sample email with a hidden request for the payroll file.

22
00:02:22,055 --> 00:02:29,608
First it shows what the scanners noticed, and what a fooled assistant would try to do.

23
00:02:29,608 --> 00:02:36,688
Then it runs the email twice. Without protection, payroll leaks. With Aegis, the forbidden actions are refused.

24
00:02:38,221 --> 00:02:45,508
We measured it on attacks written after the rules were frozen, so we couldn't tune for them.

25
00:02:45,508 --> 00:02:51,411
Attack success fell from 100% without protection to 10% with Aegis, with zero unauthorised actions.

26
00:02:51,411 --> 00:02:58,307
Every normal task still completed. 322 tests pass, and the tests caught all 26 planted security bugs.

27
00:02:59,198 --> 00:03:03,338
The AI can be completely fooled. Aegis is the wall.

28
00:03:03,338 --> 00:03:07,041
Aegis · github.com/Luci-XO/Aegis
